 tory  now voting labor that's what we need to  do to form the next government and that  is what is happening  well you you just said a rather  interesting thing you said that um  people had lent  labour had lent the liberal democrats uh  their vote which presumably means that  general election uh they intend to uh  cash it cash those votes in and and take  them back so what you're essentially  saying and  pretty much everybody uh who is expert  in this agrees with this that what we  saw this week was the return of tactical  voting  why not why doesn't labor  look at the reality  and  start to think about at least an  unofficial pact between you and the lib  dems if what you really really want to  do as you and your colleagues say every  single day is get rid of this  whatever you describe it as conservative  government  i don't think the voters of britain want  politicians to stitch up deals  in the corridors of power people expect  to put their  make their own judgment  and decide who they want to vote for  in the end in the 22 years that i've  been in public life it's clear to me  that we have two major  political parties those political  parties are actually a coalition  in of themselves and for the labour  party to form the next government we  have to pick up votes from people who  are considering voting conservative  that's the basis on which we'll form the  next government i see  no possibility of us forming the next  government by picking up a few lib dem  votes i've got to be honest about that  or indeed green votes and that is why  i'm really pleased about what happened  in wakefield on thursday  okay so no deal with liberal democrats  but at one place you are absolutely  certain not to pick up votes on the  basis of both the polls but also the  actual votes that have taken place in  local elections and so on is scotland um  are you are you ready for  a minority labor government in alliance  with the snp  well actually  we've seen an increase in our position  in scotland under and asawa really  pleased to see us winning again in  glasgow uh in the elections just a few  weeks ago so i don't recognize that look  in the end trevor you're never going to  go back to where neighbour was in  scotland come on  you were the dominant forces that isn't  going to happen trevor trevor  trevor that's a very categorical  statement uh and and i've got to say uh  this is a matter for the electorate what  we're seeing right across the electorate  have been saying for the last 15 years  they they could swing in  they've been saying for the last 15  years we're making the snp  the government the snp will get 40 50  seats in the house of commons i mean  electorates are pretty clear where they  are  trevor  i'm not going to prejudge the outcome of  an election that's likely  in two years time  let's see where we get to but on that  result in wakefield and indeed in  tibetan we would be forming the next  government with a comfortable majority  that's what that result tells us  so you're not telling me no deal with  the snp  now  we've absolutely clear there is not  we're not striking deals with the snp or  the liberal democrats we want to win  outright and we will be fighting for  every single vote in every single  constituency right across the country  all right well let's talk about how you  win and part of this is  what do people think labor stands for  last sunday on this program mick lynch  of the rmt told my colleague sophie  ridge and i'm quoting here  i don't know where labour are if someone  could tell where labour are i'd be very  happy to hear it i think they're  triangulating between what they think is  public opinion in the light of the daily  mail and the commentary in this country  and i you know i can't do mr lynch's  voice but those are his words um  could you clarify for mick lynch i'm  sure he's watching  where does labour stand on his members  dispute and the claims particularly do  you back the seven percent rise and the  demand for no compulsory redundancies  well the truth is in the name we are the  labour party  uh where a party that's always been  concerned uh for working people's  interests working people who are using  our transport up and down the country  who don't want to see strikes and of  course working people working within the  railways  in the end we're not in government this  is a matter for the government network  rail and the unions themselves and what  we've been absolutely clear on is the  government should be leading and  encouraging negotiations not stoking  this dispute for their own political  reasons that's what's happening it's not  for labor to determine the outcome of  this but it is for us to say get around  the table negotiate none of us want to  see anger strikes  i didn't ask you what you'd do if you  were in government i asked you whether  you su whether the labour party supports  the demands of another part of the labor  movement for a seven percent pay  increase and the guarantee of no  compulsory redundancies that's nothing  to do with it whether you're in  government or not it is whether you  support the demands of another labor  movement institution what is the answer  yes or no  well just to say i i don't want  confusion for people listening the rmt  is not affiliated  to the labour party oh yeah the yeah  part of the labor movement  i've been clear trevor that it is not  for labor to predetermine the outcome of  this of course we're in a cost of living  crisis there'll be workers up and down  the country who want to see an interest  in their pay and this is not just about  pay it's also about redundancies uh it's  also about conditions there are a raft  of issues that workers are concerned  with right across the country it's not  for labor to intervene in each one and  pretend that we can negotiate or that we  are on the detail of that we aren't is  the truth of this and if we were in  government we would get around the table  we would not 